::PC_U03_L06_GP01::
Question ID: PC_U03_L06_GP01
MLA Standard: MLA.PC.TR.13
Question:
What is the amplitude of y = 5sin(x)?
{
=A. 5#Correct. The coefficient 5 gives the amplitude.
~B. 2pi#2pi is the basic sine period, not amplitude.
~C. 1#This is the amplitude of y=sin(x), not y=5sin(x).
~D. 0#A sine graph with coefficient 5 does not have amplitude 0.
}
::PC_U03_L06_GP02::
Question ID: PC_U03_L06_GP02
MLA Standard: MLA.PC.TR.13
Question:
What is the period of y = sin(x)?
{
~A. pi#pi is half of the basic sine cycle.
=B. 2pi#Correct. The basic sine function has period 2pi.
~C. 1#This is not the sine period.
~D. 0#A period cannot be 0 for sine.
}
::PC_U03_L06_GP03::
Question ID: PC_U03_L06_GP03
MLA Standard: MLA.PC.TR.13
Question:
What is the midline of y = 2cos(x) + 3?
{
~A. y = 2#This is the amplitude, not the midline.
~B. y = 0#This would be the midline before the vertical shift.
=C. y = 3#Correct. The +3 shifts the graph so the midline is y=3.
~D. x = 3#A midline for sine or cosine is horizontal, so it is written y=value.
}
::PC_U03_L06_GP04::
Question ID: PC_U03_L06_GP04
MLA Standard: MLA.PC.TR.13
Question:
Which function has vertical asymptotes?
{
~A. y = sin(x)#Sine has no vertical asymptotes.
~B. y = cos(x)#Cosine has no vertical asymptotes.
~C. y = 2sin(x)#Changing sine amplitude does not create vertical asymptotes.
=D. y = tan(x)#Correct. Tangent has vertical asymptotes where cosine is 0.
}
::PC_U03_L06_GP05::
Question ID: PC_U03_L06_GP05
MLA Standard: MLA.PC.TR.13
Question:
What is the range of y = 4sin(x)?
{
=A. -4 <= y <= 4#Correct. Amplitude 4 gives outputs from -4 to 4.
~B. 0 <= y <= 4#This ignores the negative half of the sine wave.
~C. all real numbers#Sine has a bounded range.
~D. y >= 4#This describes only values above 4, which is not the sine range.
}